                <pb facs="0196" n="98" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg98"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002 Manchuria/89: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, [<date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-06-22">June 22, 1932</date>.]<lb/>[Received June 22—8:50
                        a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>694. Following from American Consul General at Mukden: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“June 21, 5 p.m. Supplementing my June 21, 4 p.m., it is apparent that
                            Changchun is in dire need of funds and that the interested authorities
                            are making every effort to evolve a practicable plan to secure the
                            customs revenue. It is thought that the settlement of the difficult
                            problem presented by the Dairen customs may hasten recognition by
                            Japan.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
